    "summary": "Pointer cycle keys conflate different pointer types at the same address",
    "failure_scenario": "For `type S struct { Value int; Ref *int }`, create `s := &S{Value: 1}` and set `s.Ref = &s.Value`. The first field starts at the same address as `s`, so the root `*S` and `Ref`'s `*int` produce the same `{kind: reflect.Ptr, ptr: ...}` key. `checkForCyclesReference` reports a cycle, although the encoder would otherwise safely emit both values and no reference leads back to `S`."
